from jsonb_to_recordset($json$[{"id":"che-st-226","chapter_id":"chemistry-stoichiometry","subject_id":"chemistry","stem":"The balanced acidic half-change $\\mathrm{MnO_4^-} + 8\\mathrm{H^+} + n\\,e^- \\rightarrow \\mathrm{Mn^{2+}} + 4\\mathrm{H_2O}$ requires $n$ equal to","options":["3","4","5","8"],"answer_index":2,"explanation":"Charge balance and the $+7 \\rightarrow +2$ change both fix $n = 5$.","images":[],"source":{"name":"ASTRA chemistry practice set","license":"Original syllabus-based practice questions (exam-frequent themes; not verbatim past papers)"},"difficulty":"medium","concept_id":"redox-balancing","appear_count":0,"years":[],"exams":[],"verified":false},{"id":"che-st-227","chapter_id":"chemistry-stoichiometry","subject_id":"chemistry","stem":"Titration of $\\mathrm{Fe^{2+}}$ with acidic $\\mathrm{MnO_4^-}$ uses the mole ratio $\\mathrm{MnO_4^-} : \\mathrm{Fe^{2+}}$ equal to","options":["1 : 1","1 : 5","5 : 1","1 : 2"],"answer_index":1,"explanation":"One $\\mathrm{MnO_4^-}$ takes five electrons; each $\\mathrm{Fe^{2+}}$ supplies one, so five Fe$^{2+}$ are needed.","images":[],"source":{"name":"ASTRA chemistry practice set","license":"Original syllabus-based practice questions (exam-frequent themes; not verbatim past papers)"},"difficulty":"medium","concept_id":"redox-balancing","appear_count":0,"years":[],"exams":[],"verified":false},{"id":"che-st-228","chapter_id":"chemistry-stoichiometry","subject_id":"chemistry","stem":"In acid, dichromate oxidises $\\mathrm{H_2S}$ to sulphur: $\\mathrm{Cr_2O_7^{2-}} + 3\\mathrm{H_2S} + 8\\mathrm{H^+} \\rightarrow 2\\mathrm{Cr^{3+}} + 3\\mathrm{S} + 7\\mathrm{H_2O}$. The coefficient 3 in front of $\\mathrm{H_2S}$ appears because","options":["each H$_2$S loses 2 e$^-$ and dichromate gains 6 e$^-$","sulphur has atomic number 16","three oxygen atoms are present in dichromate","the reaction is not redox"],"answer_index":0,"explanation":"H$_2$S (S = $-2$ to 0) is a 2-electron reductant; Cr$_2$O$_7^{2-}$ is a 6-electron oxidant, so three H$_2$S balance one dichromate.","images":[],"source":{"name":"ASTRA chemistry practice set","license":"Original syllabus-based practice questions (exam-frequent themes; not verbatim past papers)"},"difficulty":"hard","concept_id":"redox-balancing","appear_count":0,"years":[],"exams":[],"verified":false},{"id":"che-st-229","chapter_id":"chemistry-stoichiometry","subject_id":"chemistry","stem":"Gram-equivalent mass of a substance is obtained from","options":["molar mass $\\times$ $n$-factor","molar mass / $n$-factor","molar mass + $n$-factor","molarity / $n$-factor"],"answer_index":1,"explanation":"Equivalent mass $= M / n$, where $n$ is the $n$-factor of the reaction.","images":[],"source":{"name":"ASTRA chemistry practice set","license":"Original syllabus-based practice questions (exam-frequent themes; not verbatim past papers)"},"difficulty":"easy","concept_id":"equivalents-normality","appear_count":0,"years":[],"exams":[],"verified":false},{"id":"che-st-230","chapter_id":"chemistry-stoichiometry","subject_id":"chemistry","stem":"Hydrochloric acid donates one H$^+$ per formula unit, so its $n$-factor in acid–base work is","options":["1","2","3","0"],"answer_index":0,"explanation":"Monobasic HCl has $n = 1$.","images":[],"source":{"name":"ASTRA chemistry practice set","license":"Original syllabus-based practice questions (exam-frequent themes; not verbatim past papers)"},"difficulty":"easy","concept_id":"equivalents-normality","appear_count":0,"years":[],"exams":[],"verified":false},{"id":"che-st-231","chapter_id":"chemistry-stoichiometry","subject_id":"chemistry","stem":"Sulphuric acid is dibasic. In complete neutralisation its $n$-factor is","options":["1","2","3","4"],"answer_index":1,"explanation":"H$_2$SO$_4$ can furnish two H$^+$ ions, so $n = 2$.","images":[],"source":{"name":"ASTRA chemistry practice set","license":"Original syllabus-based practice questions (exam-frequent themes; not verbatim past papers)"},"difficulty":"easy","concept_id":"equivalents-normality","appear_count":0,"years":[],"exams":[],"verified":false},{"id":"che-st-232","chapter_id":"chemistry-stoichiometry","subject_id":"chemistry","stem":"Normality $N$ and molarity $M$ of the same solution are related by","options":["$N = M / n$","$N = M \\times n$","$N = M + n$","$N = n / M$"],"answer_index":1,"explanation":"Each mole contributes $n$ equivalents, so $N = M \\times n$.","images":[],"source":{"name":"ASTRA chemistry practice set","license":"Original syllabus-based practice questions (exam-frequent themes; not verbatim past papers)"},"difficulty":"easy","concept_id":"equivalents-normality","appear_count":0,"years":[],"exams":[],"verified":false},{"id":"che-st-233","chapter_id":"chemistry-stoichiometry","subject_id":"chemistry","stem":"Sodium hydroxide has molar mass 40 g mol$^{-1}$ and $n = 1$. Its gram-equivalent mass is","options":["20 g eq$^{-1}$","40 g eq$^{-1}$","80 g eq$^{-1}$","23 g eq$^{-1}$"],"answer_index":1,"explanation":"For a monoacidic base, equivalent mass equals the molar mass, 40 g eq$^{-1}$.","images":[],"source":{"name":"ASTRA chemistry practice set","license":"Original syllabus-based practice questions (exam-frequent themes; not verbatim past papers)"},"difficulty":"easy","concept_id":"equivalents-normality","appear_count":0,"years":[],"exams":[],"verified":false},{"id":"che-st-234","chapter_id":"chemistry-stoichiometry","subject_id":"chemistry","stem":"Calcium hydroxide $\\mathrm{Ca(OH)_2}$ is a diacidic base, so its $n$-factor is","options":["1","2","3","4"],"answer_index":1,"explanation":"Two OH$^-$ ions per formula unit give $n = 2$.","images":[],"source":{"name":"ASTRA chemistry practice set","license":"Original syllabus-based practice questions (exam-frequent themes; not verbatim past papers)"},"difficulty":"easy","concept_id":"equivalents-normality","appear_count":0,"years":[],"exams":[],"verified":false},{"id":"che-st-235","chapter_id":"chemistry-stoichiometry","subject_id":"chemistry","stem":"The number of gram-equivalents in a weighed sample equals","options":["mass $\\times$ equivalent mass","mass / equivalent mass","molar mass / mass","volume / normality"],"answer_index":1,"explanation":"Equivalents $= m / E$, just as moles $= m / M$.","images":[],"source":{"name":"ASTRA chemistry practice set","license":"Original syllabus-based practice questions (exam-frequent themes; not verbatim past papers)"},"difficulty":"easy","concept_id":"equivalents-normality","appear_count":0,"years":[],"exams":[],"verified":false},{"id":"che-st-236","chapter_id":"chemistry-stoichiometry","subject_id":"chemistry","stem":"Complete neutralisation of sulphuric acid uses $n = 2$. The equivalent mass of $\\mathrm{H_2SO_4}$ (M = 98) is therefore","options":["24.5 g eq$^{-1}$","49 g eq$^{-1}$","98 g eq$^{-1}$","196 g eq$^{-1}$"],"answer_index":1,"explanation":"$E = 98/2 = 49$ g eq$^{-1}$.","images":[],"source":{"name":"ASTRA chemistry practice set","license":"Original syllabus-based practice questions (exam-frequent themes; not verbatim past papers)"},"difficulty":"medium","concept_id":"equivalents-normality","appear_count":0,"years":[],"exams":[],"verified":false},{"id":"che-st-237","chapter_id":"chemistry-stoichiometry","subject_id":"chemistry","stem":"A bottle labelled 0.50 M $\\mathrm{H_2SO_4}$ is used as a dibasic acid. The normality of that solution is","options":["0.25 N","0.50 N","1.0 N","2.0 N"],"answer_index":2,"explanation":"$N = M \\times n = 0.50 \\times 2 = 1.0$ N.","images":[],"source":{"name":"ASTRA chemistry practice set","license":"Original syllabus-based practice questions (exam-frequent themes; not verbatim past papers)"},"difficulty":"medium","concept_id":"equivalents-normality","appear_count":0,"years":[],"exams":[],"verified":false},{"id":"che-st-238","chapter_id":"chemistry-stoichiometry","subject_id":"chemistry","stem":"In acidic medium $\\mathrm{KMnO_4}$ is reduced to $\\mathrm{Mn^{2+}}$. The $n$-factor of permanganate in that change is","options":["1","3","5","7"],"answer_index":2,"explanation":"Mn falls from $+7$ to $+2$, a five-electron change, so $n = 5$.","images":[],"source":{"name":"ASTRA chemistry practice set","license":"Original syllabus-based practice questions (exam-frequent themes; not verbatim past papers)"},"difficulty":"medium","concept_id":"equivalents-normality","appear_count":0,"years":[],"exams":[],"verified":false},{"id":"che-st-239","chapter_id":"chemistry-stoichiometry","subject_id":"chemistry","stem":"Potassium permanganate (M = 158 g mol$^{-1}$) used in acidic titration has equivalent mass","options":["31.6 g eq$^{-1}$","52.7 g eq$^{-1}$","79.0 g eq$^{-1}$","158 g eq$^{-1}$"],"answer_index":0,"explanation":"$E = 158/5 = 31.6$ g eq$^{-1}$ for the five-electron acidic reduction.","images":[],"source":{"name":"ASTRA chemistry practice set","license":"Original syllabus-based practice questions (exam-frequent themes; not verbatim past papers)"},"difficulty":"medium","concept_id":"equivalents-normality","appear_count":0,"years":[],"exams":[],"verified":false},{"id":"che-st-240","chapter_id":"chemistry-stoichiometry","subject_id":"chemistry","stem":"Twenty-five millilitres of 0.20 N $\\mathrm{H_2SO_4}$ are titrated with 0.10 N NaOH. The volume of alkali required for complete neutralisation is","options":["12.5 mL","25 mL","50 mL","100 mL"],"answer_index":2,"explanation":"$N_1V_1 = N_2V_2$ gives $V(\\mathrm{NaOH}) = (0.20 \\times 25)/0.10 = 50$ mL.","images":[],"source":{"name":"ASTRA chemistry practice set","license":"Original syllabus-based practice questions (exam-frequent themes; not verbatim past papers)"},"difficulty":"hard","concept_id":"equivalents-normality","appear_count":0,"years":[],"exams":[],"verified":false},{"id":"che-st-241","chapter_id":"chemistry-stoichiometry","subject_id":"chemistry","stem":"A one-molar aqueous solution is prepared so that one litre of the finished solution contains","options":["1 mol of solute","1 mol of solvent only","1 g of solute","1 kg of solute"],"answer_index":0,"explanation":"Molarity is moles of solute per litre of solution; 1 M means 1 mol in 1 L of solution.","images":[],"source":{"name":"ASTRA chemistry practice set","license":"Original syllabus-based practice questions (exam-frequent themes; not verbatim past papers)"},"difficulty":"easy","concept_id":"molarity-dilution","appear_count":0,"years":[],"exams":[],"verified":false},{"id":"che-st-242","chapter_id":"chemistry-stoichiometry","subject_id":"chemistry","stem":"Half a mole of glucose is dissolved and the solution is made up to 250 mL. The molarity of that solution is","options":["0.50 M","1.0 M","2.0 M","4.0 M"],"answer_index":2,"explanation":"$M = 0.50 / 0.250 = 2.0$ mol L$^{-1}$.","images":[],"source":{"name":"ASTRA chemistry practice set","license":"Original syllabus-based practice questions (exam-frequent themes; not verbatim past papers)"},"difficulty":"easy","concept_id":"molarity-dilution","appear_count":0,"years":[],"exams":[],"verified":false},{"id":"che-st-243","chapter_id":"chemistry-stoichiometry","subject_id":"chemistry","stem":"When a solution is diluted with solvent, the product $M_1V_1$ compared with $M_2V_2$ is","options":["larger after dilution","smaller after dilution","unchanged, because solute moles stay the same","equal to the density"],"answer_index":2,"explanation":"Dilution conserves the amount of solute, so $M_1V_1 = M_2V_2$.","images":[],"source":{"name":"ASTRA chemistry practice set","license":"Original syllabus-based practice questions (exam-frequent themes; not verbatim past papers)"},"difficulty":"easy","concept_id":"molarity-dilution","appear_count":0,"years":[],"exams":[],"verified":false},{"id":"che-st-244","chapter_id":"chemistry-stoichiometry","subject_id":"chemistry","stem":"Two moles of NaOH pellets are dissolved and the solution is made up to 1.00 L. The molarity of the alkali is","options":["0.50 M","1.0 M","2.0 M","4.0 M"],"answer_index":2,"explanation":"$M = 2.0\\ \\mathrm{mol} / 1.00\\ \\mathrm{L} = 2.0$ M.","images":[],"source":{"name":"ASTRA chemistry practice set","license":"Original syllabus-based practice questions (exam-frequent themes; not verbatim past papers)"},"difficulty":"easy","concept_id":"molarity-dilution","appear_count":0,"years":[],"exams":[],"verified":false},{"id":"che-st-245","chapter_id":"chemistry-stoichiometry","subject_id":"chemistry","stem":"Four grams of NaOH (M = 40 g mol$^{-1}$) are dissolved and made up to 500 mL. The molarity of the solution is","options":["0.10 M","0.20 M","0.40 M","2.0 M"],"answer_index":1,"explanation":"Moles of NaOH $= 4/40 = 0.10$; $M = 0.10 / 0.50 = 0.20$ M.","images":[],"source":{"name":"ASTRA chemistry practice set","license":"Original syllabus-based practice questions (exam-frequent themes; not verbatim past papers)"},"difficulty":"easy","concept_id":"molarity-dilution","appear_count":0,"years":[],"exams":[],"verified":false},{"id":"che-st-246","chapter_id":"chemistry-stoichiometry","subject_id":"chemistry","stem":"A 100 mL portion of 2.0 M HCl is diluted with water to 500 mL. The molarity after dilution is","options":["0.20 M","0.40 M","1.0 M","2.0 M"],"answer_index":1,"explanation":"$M_2 = (2.0 \\times 100)/500 = 0.40$ M.","images":[],"source":{"name":"ASTRA chemistry practice set","license":"Original syllabus-based practice questions (exam-frequent themes; not verbatim past papers)"},"difficulty":"medium","concept_id":"molarity-dilution","appear_count":0,"years":[],"exams":[],"verified":false},{"id":"che-st-247","chapter_id":"chemistry-stoichiometry","subject_id":"chemistry","stem":"A student must prepare 250 mL of 1.0 M $\\mathrm{H_2SO_4}$ from a 5.0 M stock. The volume of stock that should be pipetted is","options":["25 mL","50 mL","100 mL","200 mL"],"answer_index":1,"explanation":"$V_1 = (1.0 \\times 250)/5.0 = 50$ mL of stock, then diluted to 250 mL.","images":[],"source":{"name":"ASTRA chemistry practice set","license":"Original syllabus-based practice questions (exam-frequent themes; not verbatim past papers)"},"difficulty":"medium","concept_id":"molarity-dilution","appear_count":0,"years":[],"exams":[],"verified":false},{"id":"che-st-248","chapter_id":"chemistry-stoichiometry","subject_id":"chemistry","stem":"200 mL of 0.50 M NaCl is mixed with 300 mL of 1.50 M NaCl (volumes additive). The molarity of the blended solution is","options":["0.90 M","1.00 M","1.10 M","1.20 M"],"answer_index":2,"explanation":"Solute moles $= 0.10 + 0.45 = 0.55$; total volume $= 0.50$ L, so $M = 1.10$.","images":[],"source":{"name":"ASTRA chemistry practice set","license":"Original syllabus-based practice questions (exam-frequent themes; not verbatim past papers)"},"difficulty":"hard","concept_id":"molarity-dilution","appear_count":0,"years":[],"exams":[],"verified":false}]$json$::jsonb) as x(
